  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 241 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nLow level easterly flow continues to support low stratus and \nareas of dense fog development over central and north-central MT, \nwhile a moist southwesterly flow aloft overruns the cooler air \nmass and sends waves of light showers, sprinkles, and even some \nisolated instances of freezing drizzle. This general pattern will \nmore or less persist through Sunday before a closed mid -level low\nover the Pacific NW heads towards the Northern Rockies and brings\nmore widespread rain and snow Sunday through early Monday. The \nfog impacts will be most widespread over central and north-central\nareas during the overnight and early morning hours. \n\nThe closed low will begin migrating towards the Northern Rockies \nlate Saturday night into Sunday while being weakened and elongated\nby synoptic shearing. With the colder air aloft not scheduled to \narrive until Sunday night, snow levels will be over 7000 ft for \nmost locations, except along the Continental Divide. This will \nbring mostly liquid precipitation for lower elevations with the \nexception of some isolated instances of light freezing drizzle \nover the plains west of I15. Some freezing precipitation may also \noccur for the colder southwest valleys along the MT/ID/WY borders.\n\nH700 temperatures finally fall to around -5C by Sunday night into\nearly Monday and this will bring more efficient accumulating \nsnow, heaviest over the Madison/Gallatin ranges above 6000 ft. \nThere will be a brief period with snow levels falling to the \nvalley/plains floors Monday morning, although it does not appear \nthat this will be long enough to support widespread accumulating \nsnow impacts. Northwesterly winds may get gusty Monday morning, \nthough gusts look to stay below 40 mph. Ensembles continue to \nfavor ridging aloft to bring drier conditions and near average \ntemperatures for the remaining first half of next week, with \nunsettled conditions looking to return later in the week. - RCG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\n...Periods of dense fog through Sunday...\n\nLow level easterly flow will continue to maintain low stratus and\nmostly nighttime patchy/areas of fog through Sunday. Central and \nnorth-central areas will see the most widespread instances of \ndense fog below a half mile, namely plains locations along the \nRocky Mountain Front and on the north side of the central island \nranges. Some of the colder/higher southwest valleys will also see \npatchy dense fog, especially those along the MT/ID/WY borders. \nImpacts will mostly be directly related from the reduced \nvisibility, but freezing drizzle may cause some slippery spots to \ndevelop on area roadways, particularly the more susceptible \nbridges and elevated surfaces. \n\n...Light mountain snow and lower elevation rain, snow, and some \nmixed precipitation Sunday through Monday morning...\n\nThe main trough moves through Sunday through early Monday and \nbrings a wave of widespread precipitation to much of the forecast \narea. Aside from some light snow along the Continental Divide and \nperhaps some spotty freezing drizzle over the plains west of I15 \nand over the higher southwest valleys, most of the precipitation \nwill be falling in the form of rain until Sunday night. The \noverall pattern and NBM probabilities favor the Madison and \nGallatin ranges for the best chance (60 to 80%) for snowfall \namounts in excess of 3 inches. Lighter snows, generally up to a \ncouple inches, look to fall along the Continental Divide and the \ncentral island ranges. The colder air is still not looking to be \noptimally aligned with the moisture for lower elevation snows, but\nwill monitor the Gallatin valley where there is around a 30 to \n40% chance for a couple inches of snow on the south/eastern \nfringes of the valley Monday morning. - RCG",
